If Jane is not guilty, it is fairly plausible that injuries later identified at autopsy were not present, or at least not observable, when Ben arrived at hospital.

There are several innocent possibilities:
- some findings may have arisen during resuscitation;
- some may have developed or become more apparent after death;
- some internal injuries would not have been visible to nurses on initial examination;
- and in an emergency, clinical staff may focus on resuscitation rather than detailed documentation of subtle trauma.

Also, “not noted by nurses” does not necessarily prove “not present”; it may simply mean they were missed, not externally visible, or not recorded.

So under the not-guilty hypothesis, it is moderately likely that the injuries found later were not evident or not present in recognizable form on arrival.

